<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of Canada</h1><p class="st-meta">1841 CE – 1867 CE</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British colony that united Upper and Lower Canada in 1841, won responsible government, and ended in deadlock that produced Confederation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Following the rebellions of 1837 and 1838 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lambton-1st-earl-of-durham">Lord Durham's</a> report, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-union-1840">Act of Union</a> of 1840 merged <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/upper-canada">Upper Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lower-canada">Lower Canada</a> into a single province, proclaimed in February 1841. The two halves, known as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/canada">Canada</a> West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada-east">Canada East</a>, received equal seats in the assembly despite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> East's larger population.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The capital moved between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingston-ontario">Kingston</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec City</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> in 1857. Reformers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-baldwin">Robert Baldwin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-hippolyte-lafontaine">Louis-Hippolyte LaFontaine</a> achieved responsible government in 1848, confirmed when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce-8th-earl-of-elgin">Lord Elgin</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-losses-bill">Rebellion Losses Bill</a> despite riots that burned Montreal's parliament in 1849. Canals, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-trunk-railway">Grand Trunk Railway</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canadian-american-reciprocity-treaty">Reciprocity Treaty</a> of 1854 with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> boosted trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the 1860s sectional rivalry made stable government impossible. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald">John A. Macdonald</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-etienne-cartier">George-Étienne Cartier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-brown-canadian-politician">George Brown</a> formed the Great Coalition of 1864 to pursue federation, and in 1867 the province was split into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-quebec">Quebec</a> within the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/canada">Canada</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-canada.jpg" alt="Province of Canada" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(1-2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1841 CE – 1867 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John A. Macdonald, George-Étienne Cartier, Lord Elgin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Canada</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>United Province of Canada, Canada (1841-1867)</dd></dl></aside></div>
